Among many applications, the application of MOF as a fluorescent sensing material is one of its most important applications.17,18 MOF-based luminescence sensors have attracted great attention due to the following advantages: (i) compared with gas chromatography/mass spectrometry (GC/ MS) or high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) methods, the equipment to measure the luminescence intensities of MOF-based luminescence sensors are facile, inexpensive, and easy to operate; (ii) the MOF material has functional diversity and structural adjustability, and the luminescence and chemical as well as physical properties can be changed by changing the metal ions and organic ligands used for constructing the MOF to realize a luminescence sensor as a specific target species;19,20 (iii) a MOF with a high specific surface area can concentrate the analyte, thereby increasing the sensitivity of the detection; (iv) a MOF exhibits good recyclability and flexible porosity that limits the distance of analyte to the MOF and ensures interaction between the MOF and the analyte species.17,21−23 In addition, porosity can support the adsorption of analyte in MOF pores, resulting in preconcentration of analyte at the sensor and significant improvement in sensing performance.24 Based on the above advantages, different MOFs have been synthesized as luminescent detection platforms for various target analyses,25 such as ions (especially metal cations),26−29 gases,30−32 organic small molecules,33−35 explosives,17,36,37 temperature,38−40 humidity,41−43 and pH.44−47
MOFs have abundant luminescence centers, and their luminescence mechanisms include luminescence of metal ions, luminescence of organic ligands, and luminescence of electron transfer.18,36,48 The luminescence of metal ions is mainly centered on lanthanide ions, and the direct excitation luminescence of lanthanide ions is very weak due to the existence of $_\mathrm{f-f}$ transition inhibition of lanthanide ions. The radiant energy is then absorbed directly by coordinating a suitable organic ligand and then transferred to the lanthanide ions to sensitize the luminescence of the lanthanide ions, which is the so-called “antenna effect”.49,50 The process requires that the lowest triplet $T_{1}$ energy level of the direct absorption of the ligand is higher than the resonance energy of the lanthanide ions. Since the luminescence of the lanthanide ion comes from the electron transition in the inner layer, the lanthanide metal− organic framework material (Ln-MOF) is characterized by high purity and is not easily disturbed by the environment. The luminescence of organic ligands includes two types: fluorescence and phosphorescence. The ligand absorbs photons with appropriate energy, and the electron transitions from the ground state $S_{0}$ to the singlet excited state $S_{1}$ . During the transition of the electron back to the ground state $S_{0},$ the energy is released in the form of fluorescence. The phosphorescence is that the electrons in $S_{1}$ reach the triplet state $T_{1}$ through the intersystem channel and then transit to the ground state $S_{0},$ and the energy is released in the form of phosphorescence. Electron transfer luminescence is mainly ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) and metal-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT).22,51 These two electron transfer phenomena are common in transition-metal-based MOFs of ${\bf{\bar{\boldsymbol{d}}}}^{10}$ . LMCT is typically present in $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and Cd(II) compounds, while MLCT is typically present in $\mathrm{{Cu}(I)}$ and $\mathrm{Ag(\bar{I})}$ compounds.52,53 In addition to the luminescence of MOF itself, the researchers also realized the luminescence of MOFs by introducing luminescent guests into the metal− organic framework. Due to their inorganic−organic hybrid nature, MOF materials can be widely combined by the inherent physical and chemical properties of inorganic and organic photonic units. Second, the synergistic effect among multiple photonic units (MPUs) allows the MOF not only to retain the inherent characteristics of each photonic unit but also to generate new photonic functions through further improvement and optimization.54 In addition, the pore size and shape of the MOF can be easily adjusted to match different luminescent guests (Figure 1). The MOF materials have the advantages of broad binding possibility, synergistic effect, and controllable and orderly arrangement, making them different from other inorganic and organic photonic materials and become a good platform for the incorporation of luminescent guests. In this Review, we sum up some reported applications of a series of luminescence sensors constructed by doping luminescent guests such as lanthanide ions, dyes, quantum dots, and luminescent complexes into metal−organic frameworks for fluorescence detection of various target analyses, such as ions, gases, organic small molecules, explosives, temperature, and pH. In addition, the prospect of future research in this field and the still existing challenges were also discussed.
## ENCAPSULATION OF DIFFERENT LUMINESCENT GUESTS IN THE MOFS
Lanthanide Ions. Lanthanide metal ions have unique luminescent properties, a narrow emission peak, a large Stokes shift, a strong fluorescence intensity, a long lifetime, and other advantage s,55,56 which makes the worldwide research on lanthanide metal ions show an exponential upward trend in recent decades. Apart from the research on the MOF materials constructed with lanthanide metal ions as the metal center, the development of fluorescence detection by doping lanthanide ions into the MOF framework through postsynthetic modification was discussed.57 It mainly includes two kinds of fluorescence sensors constructed by a single luminescence center and a double luminescence center.
The construction method of luminescent sensors based on MOF materials with a single luminescence center is simple, and the fluorescence signal change of single emission can be easily measured. For example, Zhou et al.58 prepared a highly selective and sensitive nano fluorescent probe for detecting $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions (Figure 2A) in aqueous solution by encapsulating ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions in the pores of MIL-53-COOH(Al) nanocrystals. Xu et al.59 encapsulated ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in MIL-124 pores, and the synthesized $\mathrm{Eu}^{-3+}@\mathrm{MIL}.124$ $(\mathrm{Ga}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{C}_{9}\mathrm{O}_{6}^{-}\mathrm{H}_{4}))$ showed a remarkable luminescence quenching effect on $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . In addition, it has good selectivity for metal ions $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (Figure 2B,C), anions $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-},$ , and organic small molecules acetone. Zhang et al.60 doped ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions into the crystal of MOF material UiO-66 by a hydrothermal and postsynthesis modification method, and the synthesized $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66$ has photothermal stability and fluorescence stability in aqueous solution. The existence of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ replaces ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in MOF (Figure 2D), which quenches its fluorescence and realizes high sensitivity and selectivity detection of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in aqueous solution, apart from using lanthanide-ion-doped MOF materials to directly interact with the analyte to construct fluorescence detection. Li et al.61 encapsulated a ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ complex into ZIF-8 and introduced calcium 2,6-pyridinedicarboxylate (PDA) to induce the luminescence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ . Free PDA is released after adding hypochlorite anion $\mathrm{CIO^{-}}$ , because the $\mathrm{CIO^{-}}$ has a stronger affinity with ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ . At last, it leads to the quenching of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ red fluorescence emission at $613~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . When thiocyanate $S C\mathrm{{N}^{-}}$ is added, the coordination relationship between ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and PDA appeared for the occurrence of a redox reaction between $\mathrm{CIO^{-}}$ and $S C\mathrm{N}^{-}$ , thus restoring the fluorescence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions. The quantitative detection of $\mathrm{CIO^{-}}$ and $S C\mathrm{{N}^{-}}$ in water is realized with specific sensitivity and good reversibility, which shows the potential application in anti-counterfeiting. $\mathrm{Yan}^{62}$ introduced glucose oxidase $\left(\operatorname{GOx}\right)$ for the first time to construct a logic detector using $\mathrm{GOx{-}E u}^{3+}@\mathrm{UMOF}$ as a logic platform for fluorescence detection of glucose (GIU). The introduction of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions makes the composites have remarkable fluorescence properties, and the introduction of $\mathbf{GOx}$ produces $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ through enzyme cascade catalysis, while $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ will collapse the $\bar{\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}}$ structure and cause fluorescence quenching, thus realizing accurate detection of glucose. Furthermore, the sensor can detect the content of GIU in serum and urine separately and can distinguish the results with the naked eye, which promotes the development of a luminescent MOF− enzyme complex logic sensor. In addition, some transition metal ions can generate affinity with the measured object, thus changing the luminescence response of lanthanide ions. For example, Zhang et al.63 encapsulated ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion into UiO-66- $(\mathrm{CO}\bar{\mathrm{OH}})_{2}$ crystal and then introduced transition metal ion ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ as the reaction center of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S},$ so as to introduce the reaction site of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ into the skeleton. The nano fluorescent metal− organic framework $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ag}^{+}@\mathrm{UiO}\cdot66\ –(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ can be used for early asthma detection (Figure 2E). The existence of ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ sensitized the luminescence of $\mathrm{\bar{E}u}^{3+}$ . When detecting $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ has a strong affinity to ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ , which weakens the fluorescence of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ag}^{+}@\mathrm{UiO}\cdot6\dot{6}\ –(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ . Qian and his team64 codoped ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and transition metal ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ into UiO-67-bpydc (bpydc $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- ${\boldsymbol{\it\Omega}}5{\mathrm{,}}5^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylic acid) through postsynthesis modification. The new MOF material $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{-2+}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}67\mathrm{-}$ bpydc can be used to detect glutathione (GSH) in serum. The existence of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions will quench the luminescence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions. As a soft acid, ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ has a strong affinity for biological sulfhydryl molecules, which will interact with $\mathrm{GSH},$ thus weakening the quenching effect on ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ (Figure 2F). A GSH sensor with good selectivity, sensitivity, and anti-interference ability is realized.
In addition to the doping of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions above, other common lanthanide ions such as $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ were also frequently investigated by the researchers. Ji et al.65 doped $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions into $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF by postsynthesis. The synthesized $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@\mathrm{Zn}{\cdot}\mathrm{MOF}$ could rapidly and selectively respond to $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ ions in living cell buffer within $10\ s.$ . Qin et al.66 prepared an economical and efficient portable medical method for detecting aniline metabolites in human urine by modifying $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ to $\mathrm{A\bar{l}(O H)(b p y d c)}.$ , and the fluorescence intensity of the synthesized $\bar{\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}@\mathrm{Al(OH)}$ - (bpydc) decreased with the increase of $p$ -aminophenol (PAP). Cui et al.67 prepared a method for the determination of cystine in urine by encapsulating $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ in Cd-MOF $\mathrm{(}[\mathrm{Cd}_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{L}){\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ , $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{L}=5,5^{\prime}$ -( $_{1H-1,2,4}$ -triazole3,5-diyl) diisophthalic acid). Pan et al.68 doped $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions into
$Z\mathrm{n}$ -PDC $(\mathrm{[Zn(}2,\mathsf{S}\mathrm{-PDC)(H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}_{2}\mathrm{]}\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ by a simple room temperature precipitation method. When the antibiotic cefixime (CFX) is added, the fluorescence material has a stronger LUMO energy level than the analyte, which leads to electron transfer. At the same time, their absorption peaks overlap greatly, resulting in an internal filtration effect (IFE), thus quenching the fluorescence of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions. The material shows a high sensitivity and selective response to the antibiotic CFX. All of these are based on the fluorescence quenching response to realize the detection of analyte. In comparison, the fluorescence probe based on “turn on” has a higher antiinterference ability. For example, Zheng et al.69 prepared a probe with fluorescence-on response to $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ions in aqueous solution based on the coordination functionalized $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF with $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . The probe has high selectivity, low detection limit, and good anti-interference ability. The enhancement may be attributed to the Lewis acid−base interaction between $\mathrm{F^{-}}$ and ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ , which allows energy transfer from the ligand to $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . The presence of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ not only regulates the luminescence of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ but also provides an available site for interaction with the analyte. For example, Sun et al.70 designed a novel luminescent probe based on $\mathrm{\hat{T}b}^{3+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}.$ modified MOF-1 $\mathrm{(Ga(OH)(btec)}$ · $0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\rangle$ ) for the selective detection of creatine in urine. The strong interaction between ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and sarcosine can restore the fluorescence of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , realizing an “on−off−on” process for the detection of sarcosine. Liu et al.71 postdoped $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ in the prepared $\mathtt{C u}$ -BTC. Similarly, the luminescent intensity of the synthesized $\mathrm{Cu-BTC/Tb^{3+}}$ is weak due to the quenching effect of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ on $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ . When $\beta\mathrm{.}$ -peptide $(\mathsf{A}\beta)$ is added, $\mathbb{A}\beta_{1-40}$ will interact with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and thus restore the fluorescence of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions to achieve specific detection of $\beta$ -peptide.
At present, the single-emission intensity depends on many uncontrollable factors, such as light source intensity, concentration inhomogeneity, and excitation power fluctuations, which leads to quantization error.48 Thus, researchers introduce two or more luminescent centers to construct ratio fluorescent probes. The shortcomings of single-emission centers are eliminated by self-calibration of two emission bands, so as to avoid false response and improve signal-tonoise ratio, thus building a more accurate and reliable fluorescence sensor.72 The two emission centers of the ratio fluorescence sensor mainly come from bimetallic emission or double emission of lanthanide metal ions and organic ligands. Zhou and $\mathrm{Yan}^{73}$ incorporated ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ into the framework of UiO-bpydc MOF with strong ligand center emission through postsynthesis modification. $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}@$ UiO-bpydc shows double luminescence centers of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion $\left(614\ \mathrm{~nm}\right)$ and intrinsic organic ligand $\left(530\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ . When the temperature rises, the emission intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion decreases, while the emission intensity of ligand increases, showing a good linear relationship in the temperature range 293−353 K. A novel ratio temperature sensor is realized. Later, they found that the two emission intensity ratios of the MOF composites depend on different volatile organic compounds $(\mathrm{VOCs}),^{74}$ and the luminous quantum yield also changes with the adjustment of VOCs. Thus, VOCs can be detected by a double response of luminous intensity ratio and luminous quantum yield, which is more accurate and reliable. Introducing transition metal ions to realize the fluorescence detection of the measured object is not only suitable for single-emission sensors but also suitable for ratio fluorescence sensors. For example, in the detection of hydrogen sulfide, Zhang et al.75 first encapsulated the luminescent lanthanide metal ion ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in the metal−organic framework UiO-66- $(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ crystal with high thermal stability and high chemical stability and then introduced the transition metal ion ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , thus obtaining a new ratiometric luminescence sensor $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}@\mathrm{UiO}\cdot6\bar{6}\ –(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ for detecting $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ content in aqueous solution. Zheng et al.76 encapsulated lanthanide metal ion $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ into the transition metal organic framework $[\mathrm{Cu(HCPOC)}_{2}]_{n}(\mathrm{Cu1})$ and designed a luminescent colorimetric probe $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}@\mathrm{Cul}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ detection (Figure 2G). They all first use ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ to quench the luminescence of lanthanide ion. When $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is added, $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ has a strong affinity to ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , thus restoring the luminescence of lanthanide ions and inhibiting the emission of wide ligands, and the rapid detection of $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ is realized. In addition, Wang et al.77 also prepared a dual-emission fluorescent MOF material $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{TFA}\bar{@}\bar{\mathrm{MIL}}.140\mathrm{C}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}S$ detection. The addition of secondary sensitizer 1,1,1-trifluoro- $^{\cdot2,4}$ -pentanedione (TFA) can sensitize the luminous intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions and enhance the fluorescence life of the composite, which makes the fluorescence change more obvious. Transition metal ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ can also be used to encapsulate into MOFs to detect formaldehyde (FA). Hao et al.78 codoped MOF with transition metal ions ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ to prepare a dual-emission MOF nanocomposite. The injection of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ will cause ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and organic ligand in MOF to emit fluorescence simultaneously. When FA is detected, FA and ${\mathrm{Ag}}^{+}$ will undergo a redox reaction, thus enhancing the energy transfer from ligand to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and causing the opposite luminescence change of two fluorophores, which realizes the self-calibration detection of indoor formaldehyde. Due to the intense reaction between semiconductor $\mathrm{{znO}}$ and aldehyde gases, the released charge can enhance the luminescence of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ . Xu et al.79 prepared a heterostructure of semiconductor $\mathrm{{ZnO}}$ -doped UiO-MOF through a solvothermal reaction and then introduced ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ into ${\mathrm{ZnO}}_{\textcircled{a}}{\mathrm{UiO}}.$ MOF through postsynthesis, and a ligand double-emission $\left(I_{614}/I_{470}\right)$ fluorescent probe was used to detect aldehyde gases (formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, and acrolein) in automobiles. The sensor has good repeatability and the fluorescence response that can be stably exposed to $25\mathrm{-}65\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which is suitable for detection of aldehyde gases in actual automobiles. Apart from the ratio fluorescence sensor constructed by a single lanthanide metal ion and an organic ligand, it also includes a MOF fluorescence sensor constructed by double lanthanide metal ions codoping. For example, Zhou et al.80 doped two lanthanide metal ions ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ into MOF material $\ln(\mathrm{OH})$ (bpydc) through postsynthesis modification, and the synthesized $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{\bar{3}+}(\bar{\varpi}\mathrm{ln}(\bar{\mathrm{OH}})$ (bpydc) has two luminescence centers of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ $\left(613\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ and $\bar{\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}$ ( $\cdot545~\mathrm{nm})$ . When the temperature rises, the luminous intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ will increase due to the energy transfer from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , while the luminous intensity of $\mathrm{\dot{T}b}^{3+}$ will decrease, showing a good linear relationship in the temperature range $10{-}60~^{\circ}\mathrm{\bar{C}}$ . $\bar{\mathrm{Tb}}^{3+}/$ ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ -functionalized bio-MOF-1 was prepared by Zhang et al.81 through an ion exchange method, and they utilized it to construct a ratio fluorescence sensor for detecting anthrax biomarker dipicolinic acid (DPA). At first, the emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ was sensitized due to the high energy conversion from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ions to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions. When DPA is added, DPA enhances the emission of $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ at $545\ \mathrm{nm}$ through the energy transfer between ligand and metal, while the emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ at 615 nm is quenched due to the interruption of energy transfer from $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ . This sensor realizes the visual colorimetric sensing of DPA for the first time (Figure 2H).
Fluorescent Dyes. In recent years, fluorescent dyes, as cheap and easily available luminescent guests, have attracted much attention because of their high photoluminescence quantum yield, stable optical properties, wide emission wavelength range, and the potential to detect target analytes with fluorescent signal change s.82,83 However, the light stability of fluorescent dyes will be affected by the environment, and the aggregation state of dyes will greatly weaken its luminous intensity, thus limiting its application in the field of solid-state luminescent materials.84 MOF materials have an easily adjustable skeleton structure and uniform pores. Therefore, introducing fluorescent dyes into the pores of MOF materials can protect fluorescent dyes and avoid fluorescence aggregation quenching. In addition, the fluorescence properties of fluorescent dyes can be regulated by adjusting the interaction between dyes and MOFs. New fluorescence sensors based on dye@MOF have been successfully implemented.
Fluorescent dyes are introduced into non-luminous MOF materials to detect target analytes through the change of single fluorescence emission. For example, Li et al.85 successfully introduced the fluorescent molecule rhodamine 6G (R6G) into the UiO-68 skeleton from single-crystal-to-single-crystal by a postsynthesis modification method. When $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ is added, a desulfurization reaction will occur, which will lead to the ring opening of the rhodamine spirolactam ring, thus enhancing the pink fluorescence of R6G. The synthesized UiO-68-R6G can be used for the detection of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ ions in living cells or living organisms. Yan et al.86 coated the cationic dye methylene blue (MB) in $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Nicotine was added as a reducing substance, which converted MB into bright methylene blue (LB). LB had more lone pairs than MB, so the phenomenon of photoinduced electron transfer from LB to MOF was stronger, and its fluorescence intensity was greatly enhanced (Figure 3A). A fluorescence sensor was constructed for quantitative detection of nicotine in urine and living cells with fast response, high selectivity, and good water stability (Figure 3A). Zhang et al.87 doped rhodamine B (RhB) and fluorescein disodium salt (FSS) into the framework of ZIF-8 MOF material and prepared two kinds of compounds Rh ${\tt B}(\varpi Z{\tt I F}{-}8\$ and FSS@ZIF-8 which have strong emission bands at 580 and $540\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Different antibiotics were detected in aqueous solution though the fluorescence changes of $\operatorname{RhB}(\omega$ ZIF-8 and FSS@ZIF-8. It was found that nitrofuran antibiotics including nitrofurazone (NFZ) and nitrofurantoin (NFT) had an obvious quenching effect on RhB@ZIF-8 and FSS@ZIF-8, while tetracycline antibiotics such as tetracycline (TC) and oxyte-tracycline (OTC) showed obvious fluorescence enhancement on RhB@ZIF-8 and FSS@ZIF-8. And their concentration has a good linear relationship with fluorescence intensity. All of the above are achieved by simple quenching or sensitization of single fluorescence emission. Guo et al.88 first quenched the fluorescence of $\mathrm{RhB}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ by introducing 4- nitrophenyl- $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ -D-glucuronic acid (PNPG) into the synthesized RhB@MOF-5 complex, and then, the fluorescence recovery was monitored after adding $\beta$ -GCU into the system, which was attributed to the fact that the substrate of PNPG would be enzymatically hydrolyzed to $p$ -nitrophenol (PNP) and glucose. The more accurate detection of the tumor biomarker $\beta$ -GCU in serum was realized through the fluorescence quenching and recovery.
Similarly, the accuracy and precision of the method based on the change of single-emitting fluorescence intensity are often not good. Double-emitting materials successfully improve the detection accuracy and avoid the effect caused by instrument instability. A dual-emission fluorescence sensor can be constructed by using one kind of fluorescence as a detection signal and another kind of fluorescence as a reference signal. For example, Yang et al.89 encapsulated rhodamine B in the $Z\mathbf{r}.$ MOF channel to synthesize $\scriptstyle\mathrm{RhB}@\mathrm{Zr-MOF}$ which has two luminescent characteristics of dye and MOF material. RhB $@$ $_{\mathbf{Zr-MOF}}$ was used for detection of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , explosive nitroaromatic hydrocarbon (4-NP), and nitenpyram in pesticides. The detection limits were $1.6~\mu\mathrm{M}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}^{3\bar{+}}$ , $0.9\ \mu\mathrm{M}$ for 4-NP, and $0.2~\mu\mathrm{M}$ for nitenpyram. Zhang et al.90 encapsulated the fluorescent dye rhodamine B in the blue-light-emitting $Z\mathbf{r}$ - MOF skeleton. In $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ion solution, the fluorescence intensity of RhB at $420~\mathrm{nm}$ changed little, while that of DUT-52 at 607 nm decreased significantly. Therefore, the fluorescence of dye and DUT-52 was used as a reference signal and an output signal to realize the fluorescence detection of cation $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . In addition, it was found that the probe was also selective for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ . Their detection mechanism is that the absorption band of analyte completely covers the blue light emission of MOF, which promotes the resonance energy transfer from MOF to analyte and hinders the resonance energy from MOF to RhB molecule, thus quenching the fluorescence of MOF material. Chen et al.91 encapsulated rhodamine 6G (Rh6G) into $\mathrm{Zn(II){\cdot}M O F}$ $((\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{\hat{M}e}_{2})[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})(\mathrm{tpt})(\mathrm{TZB})_{3}].$ - $\left(\mathrm{DMF}\right)_{12}\right)_{n}$ $\mathrm{(tpt=2,4,6–tri}$ (4-pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TZB}=$ 4-(1H-tetrazol-5-yl) by a solvothermal method. The synthesized $\operatorname{Rh}6\mathrm{G}\ @1$ has ligand blue emission at $373~\mathrm{nm}$ and Rh6G emission at $570~\mathrm{nm}$ . Selective and sensitive detection of 2,4,6- trinitrophenol (TNP) was achieved by a MOF-based selfcalibrating sensor that relied on the dye-to-ligand emission peak height ratio as the detectable signal (Figure 3B,C). Fast response and variable luminescent colors that can be visible to the naked eye have also been successfully achieved. Du et al.92 encapsulated RhB in ZIF-8. The synthesized $\mathrm{RhB}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8$ has two kinds of luminescence: dye and ligand 2-methylimidazole. Particularly, this fluorescence sensor achieves efficient heavy metal removal with the fluorescence changing linear proportional to the adsorption capacity because of the binding of imidazole nitrogen atoms to $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , which achieves real-time removing and monitoring of copper ions.
These self-calibration fluorescence sensors are constructed with dye molecules as reference signals and fluorescence of MOF materials as detection signals. In addition, with dye fluorescence as the detection signal and MOF material as the reference signal, Li et al.93 successfully synthesized the eosin Y (EY) $|\varpi Z\mathbf{r}–\dot{\mathbf{M}}\mathrm{OF}$ composite with dual-emission characteristics using an in situ synthetic encapsulation method. The fluorescence intensity of EY is 10 times higher than that of $_{\mathbf{Zr-MOF}}$ due to the energy transfer effect between EY and $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOF. The large distinction in height and location of two emission peaks makes the EY@Zr-MOF composite act as a self-calibrating luminescent probe for detection of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ and nitroaromatics. Liu et al.94 incorporated 6-carboxyfluorescein (FAM)-labeled aptamer into the metal−organic framework PCN-222 (Figure 3D,E) for efficient and accurate detection of antibiotic chloramphenicol (CAP). The high speed and ultrasensitivity of this biosensor are attributed to the strong adsorption ability and the high quenching efficiency (QE) of the PCN-222. Among many fluorescent materials, Ln-MOF has bright visible light emission, large Stokes shift, and high quantum yield, which make it have high selectivity and low limit in detection. Li et al.95 incorporated rhodamine-derived organic dye RB into the luminescent lanthanide metal−organic framework (EuBTC). The synthesized EuBTC@RB took the emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ as a reference signal and the emission of RB dye as a sensing probe, and an optical sensing platform for detection of dipicolinate (DPA) was constructed. With the increasing concentration of DPA, the emission intensity of RB at $553~\mathrm{nm}$ was enhanced, and the emission intensity of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ at $616\ \mathrm{nm}$ was basically unchanged. The fluorescence ratio of $F_{553\mathrm{nm}}/F_{616\mathrm{nm}}$ has a good linear relationship with DPA concentration, which realizes the ratio fluorescence sensing for DPA detection. In addition, $\operatorname{EuBTC}@\operatorname{RB}$ has an obvious color change when detecting DPA, which can be observed by the naked eye. The absorption of EuBTC@RB in the visible light region is proportional to DPA concentration, which realizes the colorimetric sensing application of EuBTC@RB.
In addition to the self-calibration dual-emission fluorescence sensor, it also includes a ratio fluorescence sensor based on the common change of two fluorescence intensities. For example, using the phenomenon of double quenching or double sensitization of two kinds of fluorescence for detection, Liu et al.96 encapsulated 4-aminonaphthalimide (4-ANA) as a luminescent guest dye to host Ln-MOF and studied the photoluminescence properties of two dye@LnMOFs (4- $\mathrm{ANA@Eu}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{3},$ $4{\cdot}\mathrm{ANA}@\mathrm{Gd}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{3})$ in the temperature range $35-$ $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It is found that the two luminescence spectra of the 4- $\mathrm{ANA}@\mathrm{Gd}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{3}$ complex overlap and lead to an energy transfer. Their fluorescence intensity will decrease with the increase of temperature, and their emission intensity ratio $\left(I_{510\mathrm{nm}}/I_{420\mathrm{nm}}\right)$ has a good linear relationship with temperature. Ruan et al.9 encapsulated sulfonyl rhodamine B (SRB) in the framework of UiO-66. Doping dyes into MOF materials weakened the dye aggregation quenching phenomenon that enhances the fluorescence intensity of SRB. The synthesized SRB@UiO-66 material has double-emission centers, which can be used to detect $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ content by ratiometric fluorescence. The existence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ quenches the luminescence of SRB and UiO-66, and their fluorescence intensity ratio $\left(I_{\mathrm{UiO-}66}/I_{\mathrm{SRB}}\right)$ has a good linear relationship with $\mathrm{Fe}^{\dot{3}+}$ concentration in the range $0.1-$ $0.9\ \mathrm{mM}$ . $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ can be detected stably and sensitively in a wide $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ range. Anionic biological MOF-1 was used as a container because of its porous structure and charge properties for adsorbing the organic dye cationic acriflavine (Acf) which was reported by Liu et al.98 The synthesized Acf@biO-MOF-1 has two characteristic emission peaks of 385 and $582\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The complex Acf@biO-MOF-1 shows a fluorescence “off” phenomenon when $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ ions are contained in the aqueous solution. Particularly, Acf@bio-MOF-1 shows higher fluorescence quenching effects for $\mathrm{\mathop{Cr}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ . Fu et al.99 encapsulated the cationic dye 1-ethylpyridine salt (DSM) into the porous MOF material $[(\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\bar{H}_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{NH_{2}}][\mathrm{ln(TNB)}_{4/3}]$ · (2DMF) $\left(3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{TNB}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}\right.$ -nitrilotribenzoic acid) by the ion exchange method. The fluorescence quenching of the synthesized $\mathbf{\Pi}_{1\textcircled{a}\mathrm{DSM}}$ in the presence of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{\bar{2}-}$ and $\mathrm{H}\overline{{\mathbf{g}}}^{2+}$ ions was obviously observed (Figure 3F). It shows a double turn-off reaction, and the probe shows high sensitivity. Their detection mechanism is that the absorption band of the analyte covers the emission band of the dye $_{@\mathrm{MOF}}$ complex, resulting in a fluorescence quenching response. Yang et al.100 reported the encapsulation of rhodamine B and rhodamine 6G dyes in the MOF material: $\mathrm{\small{[Cd_{2}(t i b)(b t b)(H_{2}O)_{2}]{\cdot N O_{3}{\cdot}2D M F}}}$ (1). The two double-emission fluorescence sensors based on Rho $\mathbf{B}@1$ and Rho $6\mathrm{G}\ @1$ were used for selective detection of pesticides. With the increase of the concentration of nitenpyram, the fluorescence of both Rho $\mathbf{B}@1$ and Rho $6\mathbf{G}\textcircled{a}1$ was quenched. Wei et al.101 encapsulated EY in the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based metal−organic framework $\left(\mathbf{Zr-MOFs}\right)$ , and the prepared EY@DUT-52 composite has double-emission fluorescence characteristics of dye EY and MOF material DUT-52. The existence of nitenpyram in pesticides will destroy the energy transfer from DUT-52 to EY, which will lead to the fluorescence quenching of EY at $560\ \mathrm{nm}$ and also reduce the emission intensity of MOF itself. By using the relative fluorescence intensity instead of the absolute fluorescence intensity of emission peaks as detection signals, two EY@Zr-MOFs served as built-in self-calibrated fluorescence sensors to detect pesticides. Yu et al.102 doped RhB into $\big[\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\big]$ - $\mathrm{\bar{[Tb_{3}(d c p c p t)_{3}(H C O O)]{\cdot D M F{\cdot}}15H_{2}O(}}$ (Tb-dcpcpt) by the ion exchange method, and the synthesized RhB@Tb-dcpcpt has two emission characteristics: dye and ligand. A bifunctional sensor toward antibiotics is designed in terms of luminescent intensity and color (Figure 3G). Wang et al.103 doped the cationic dye $(E)$ -4-(4-(dimethylamino)styryl)-1-methylpyridin-1-ium (DMASM) on the metal−organic framework (bioMOF-1) by the ion exchange method, and the synthesized dye@bio-MOF-1 composite has two luminescent properties: dye and organic ligand. The complex showed an obvious phenomenon of fluorescence quenching for nitroaromatic explosives, while, for aliphatic nitro, it showed a good response of fluorescence enhancement. Selective detection of different types of trace explosives is realized.
In addition, a ratiometric fluorescence sensor can also be constructed according to the opposite fluorescence change. Li et al.104 incorporated the green dye R6G into the Eu-MOF framework with red light emission, and the synthesized $\mathrm{R6G}\textcircled{a}$ Eu-MOF has double-emission centers. The ratiometric fluorescence detection of water in aprotic and protic polar solution was discussed, respectively. Using water content as the input signal and two kinds of fluorescence emission as the output signals, a one-to-two logic gate system was constructed, thus developing an intelligence system for water detection. Zhang et al.105 presented a novel dual mode picric acid (PA) sensor by conjugating lanthanide MOFs with a rhodamine probe. The synthesized RGH-Eu (BTC) has green fluorescence emission of RGH at $555\mathrm{nm}$ and a red emission center of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion at $616~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . When the concentration of PA increases from 0 to $600~\mu\mathbf{M},$ , the emission intensity at $555~\mathrm{nm}$ will be enhanced and the fluorescence at $616~\mathrm{{nm}}$ will be quenched. This dual fluorescence emission response mechanism makes RGH-Eu (BTC) have good performance for PA detection. It displayed high sensitivity and selectivity toward PA over other nitroaromatic compounds and acidic analytes. Wang et al.106 encapsulated fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC) and Eu complex-functionalized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ into ZIF-8 to prepare a dualemission fluorescent nanoprobe, which has the emission of FITC and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ complex. It can be used for determination of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in actual samples. Because of the porous nature of ZIF-8, the aggregation of nanoparticles can be prevented, and the target analytes can be quickly located. Therefore, the probe has the advantages of good selectivity and sensitivity, low toxicity, and strong stability in a wide pH range. Wang et al.107 encapsulated anionic dye fluorescein (FS) into zinc-based MOF material $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(T i p a)_{2}(O H)]{\cdot3N O_{3}\cdot12H_{2}O}}}$ (1) (Figure 3H). The synthesized $\operatorname{FS}(\varpi1 $ has blue emission of MOF at 411 nm and green emission of FS dye at $518~\mathrm{\nm}$ . The luminescence intensity ratio of the peaks at 518 and $411~\mathrm{nm}$ well correlates linearly to the concentration of trichloroacetic acid (TCA) with a rapid response time of less than $30\ s.$ Therefore, a novel ratiometric fluorescence sensor was constructed to detect TCA, a metabolite of trichloroethylene (TCE) in urine. The detection mechanism is that the ion exchange between TCA and FS results in the collapse of crystal structure, which enhances the fluorescence of free FS ions.
Quantum Dots. Quantum dots (QDs) are semiconductor nanocrystals with a size of $2{-}10\ \mathrm{\nm},$ which have attracted much attention because of their advantages such as high fluorescence quantum yield, wide absorption bandwidth, narrow and symmetrical emission band, high extinction coefficient, and photobleaching stability.108,109 However, quantum dots may produce some agglomeration after precipitation and drying.110 At this time, by combining it with porous materials such as the metal−organic framework, electronic transition and cavitation can be suppressed, thus effectively improving the stability of quantum dots. In addition, the larger specific surface area of MOF provides a large number of binding sites for binding quantum dots. Here, we introduce some applications of encapsulation of quantum dots into MOF materials for fluorescence detection.
Inorganic Semiconductor Quantum Dots. As an excellent fluorescent probe with high fluorescence stability, inorganic semiconductor QDs@MOFs can be used to detect metal ions and small molecules. Fu et al.111 combined $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ -ion-doped $Z\mathrm{n}S$ quantum dots with metal−organic framework ZIF-8 as the fluorescence probe for detecting chromium ions $\left(\mathbf{Co}^{2+}\right)$ and dichromate $\bar{(\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-})}$ . The existence of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ quenches fluorescence. The lowest detection limits are 0.27 and $0.22~\mu\mathrm{M}$ for ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}.$ , respectively. Under the irradiation of a $254~\mathrm{nm}$ ultraviolet lamp, fluorescence can be observed with the naked eye. In the synthesis of quantum dots, surface modification with a capping agent can prevent aggregation of quantum dots, which can improve stability and luminous quantum yield. For example, Zhao et al.112 capped the capping agent 3-(aminopropyl) trimethoxysilane (APTMS) on positively charged $\mathrm{{znO}}$ quantum dots and then introduced it into the negatively charged MOF-5 skeleton to prepare a fluorescence probe ZnO@MOF-5 for detecting anionic pollutant phosphate (Pi) (Figure 4A). Flower-like complexes are formed between them due to amine−zinc interaction and electrostatic interaction, which leads to fluorescence quenching of quantum dots. When Pi ions were introduced, the structure of MOF-5 was destroyed, which inhibited the quenching effect of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ quantum dots and led to fluorescence recovery, thereby realizing efficient detection of Pi ions in tap water (Figure 4B). Asadi et al.113 selected a nontoxic surface modifier polyethylene glycol (PEG) to cap $Z\mathrm{n}S$ quantum dots and encapsulate them in ZIF-67 crystal and prepared a new fluorescent probe PEG- ${\mathbf{\cdot}}Z{\mathbf{n}}S$ QD@ZIF-67 for detecting ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ion content in water. ZIF-67 exists to adsorb ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . The bonding between ZIF-67 and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ can be measured by the change of the fluorescence signals for PEG-ZnS QDs. The sensor has high sensitivity and selectivity, has a low detection limit, and can detect ${\dot{\mathrm{Cu}}}^{2+}$ in a wide range of $3-500$ nM.
Besides using single quantum dot luminescence for detection, quantum dots can be combined with other fluorescence probes to form a ratiometric fluorescence sensor with self-calibration function, thus eliminating the interference of instruments and experimental environment. For example, Jin et al.114 combined the MOF material $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) with CdTe quantum dots terminated with 6-mercaptopropionic acid to prepare a fluorescence probe for selective detection of 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP). The sensing material has blue emission of MOF ${}^{'}\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=425~\mathrm{nm}{},$ ) and green emission of CdTe quantum dots $\left(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=528\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ . When 6-MP is added, blue fluorescence is quenched due to the strong internal filtering effect (IFES) between 6-MP and $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{MIL}.53\mathrm{(Al)}$ . The sulfhydryl group of 6-MP will bind to cadmium ions of quantum dots, thus improving the passivation of quantum dots and sensitizing the green fluorescence emission of quantum dots. The ratiometric detection of 6-MP with high detection sensitivity and selectivity was realized using the two different fluorescence changes (Figure 4C). Yang et al.115 constructed a ratiometric fluorescence sensor $(I_{425}/I_{605})$ by using CdTe quantum dots and MOF through a simple physical mixing method to detect the content of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in water or drinks. Orange-red CdTe quantum dots $\left(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=605~\mathrm{nm}\right)$ ) were quenched with the increase of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , while blue-red MOF $(\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}~=~425~\mathrm{nm})$ remain unchanged. With CdTe quantum dots as the detection signal and MOF as the internal standard, the content of $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ in samples can be analyzed on site, quickly and semiquantitatively by comparing the color changes of standard samples and actual samples. In addition to using MOF as an internal standard, Li et al.116 encapsulated luminescent CdS quantum dots and R6G fluorescent dye into ZIF-8 nanocrystals and prepared a ratiodependent fluorescent probe for detecting 2,6-pyridinedicarboxylic acid (DPA) (Figure 4D), the main component of bacterial spores. ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions are introduced to quench the fluorescence signal of quantum dots. When excited at $460~\mathrm{nm}$ , the fluorescence intensity of quantum dots at $655\ \mathrm{nm}$ will gradually recover with the increase of DPA concentration. At this time, the emission intensity of the organic dye at $549~\mathrm{nm}$ remained unchanged (Figure 4E). Quantum dots are used as the detection signal for DPA, and dyes are used as the internal reference to eliminate the influence of environmental changes on detection. According to the change of double-emission fluorescence intensity, DPA can be accurately detected in the detection range of $0.1{-}150~\mu\mathrm{M}$ .
Carbon Quantum Dots. Semiconductor quantum dots usually contain elements of lead, cadmium, and silicon, which have heavy metal toxicity and high cost, thus limiting their application. Carbon quantum dots (CQDs), as a new type of luminescent nano materials, are characterized by their unique photoluminescence stability, simple preparation, good biocompatibility, low toxicity, and inherent selectivity for analytes,117 which have been widely used to construct various fluorescent probes and sensors.118
Feng et al.119 used electrophoretic deposition (EPD) technology to encapsulate CQDs in UiO-66- $(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ and prepared a thin film for temperature detection. With the increase of temperature, the luminous intensity of carbon dots decreased, and the detection linear range was 97−297 K (Figure 5A). Xu et al.120 added carbon dots (CDs) with high quantum yield into the growth medium of ZIF-8 by a simple “one-pot method”, which made CDs directly enter the skeleton in the reaction process. The synthesized $\mathrm{CDs}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ can selectively capture and aggregate quercetin (QCT). This absorption may be attributed to the hydrogen bond and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction between $\mathrm{CDs}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ and QCT. In 2019, they prepared surface molecularly imprinted polymer (MIP) on the basis of the CDs@ZIF-8 complex.121 The synthesized $\mathrm{CD}s@\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{MIP}$ not only has higher selectivity and sensitivity to QCT but also has a faster reaction rate and a lower detection limit. In order to enhance the stability of carbon dots in the synthesis process, Lin et al.122 capped CQDs with branched poly(ethylenimine) (BPEI) and then encapsulated it in metal−organic framework ZIF-8, and synthesized a new high fluorescence composite material BPEI-CQDs/ZIF-8. ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\smash{\breve{2}}+\mathnormal{}}$ reacts with amino groups on the surface of BPEI-CQDs, forming an inactive amine−copper complex, which leads to fluorescence quenching of CQDs, thus realizing a ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sensor with high sensitivity, high selectivity, wide detection range, and low detection limit for detection in environmental water samples. Moreover, the sensor can adsorb and enrich ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ (Figure 5B), which can greatly amplify the sensing signal and specificity. In addition, Yang et al.123 immobilized amino-functionalized carbon dots (amine-CQDs) on UiO-66 MOF material by the postsynthesis modification method and used UiO-66 as an adsorbent to selectively capture target molecules. The binding interaction between UiO-66 and the target analyte can be studied by monitoring the fluorescence signal change of amine-CQDs. The amine$\mathrm{CQDs@UiO–66}$ can be used to detect 4-nitrophenol (4-NP) with high selectivity and sensitivity in the range $0.01{-}20.0\mu\mathrm{M}$ .
In addition to using the single fluorescence emission change of carbon dots for detection, Jalili et al.124 encapsulated the blue carbon dots (BCDs) and yellow carbon dots (YCDs) with blue and yellow fluorescence into the ZIF-8 frame by the “one pot method”. The synthesized BYCDs@ZIF-8 combines the fluorescence characteristics of two different colors of carbon dots and can be used for ratiometric fluorescence detection of ratiometric fluorescence sensor by doping carbon dots and other luminescent guests into MOF materials. Among them, lanthanide ions and carbon dots are the most common luminescent guests. For example, Dong et al.125 synthesized a novel double-emission nanohybrid Eu-MOFs/N,S-CDs by encapsulating $_{\mathrm{N-S}}$ codoped carbon dots (N,S-CDs) with blue emission into the lanthanide metal−organic framework Eu-MOFs with red emission. The hybrid shows red light emission in organic solution, while, in aqueous solution, the encapsulated N,S-CDs are released into the solution due to $_\mathrm{O-H}$ oscillation, and the hybrid shows blue emission because the red emission of Eu-MOFs is quenched, so it can be used as a nano probe for detection of water content in organic solution. Wu et al.126 codoped red-emitting carbon dots (pCDs) and green-emitting lanthanide metal ion $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ into MOF to prepare the dual-emission colorimetric fluorescent probe $\mathrm{Tb}^{\hat{3}+}\hat{@\mathrm{p}}{\mathrm{-CDs/MOF}}$ (Figure 5C). When the probe is in organic solution (ethanol), p-CDs have stronger luminous intensity and show red luminescence emission. Due to the aggregation effect of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p\}}$ -CDs in water, only green luminescence emission is displayed because red luminescence emission is quenched (Figure 5D). Therefore, the probe can be used to detect the water content in ethanol and can also be used as a colorimetric luminescence sensor for measuring relative humidity. The ratio MOF fluorescence sensor constructed by lanthanide ions and carbon dots is also suitable for detection of ions. Xu et al.127 encapsulated CDs with blue luminescence emission and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ion with red luminescence emission into MOF-253 crystal. The blue luminescence emission of CDs is quenched because CDs can chelate with $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ to form a stable chelate, thus only showing red luminescence emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ . A novel ratiometric $\mathrm{Hg}^{2\mp}$ fluorescence sensor is constructed that can be applied to detect $\mathrm{Hg}^{2+}$ in environmental water samples (Figure 5E). Hao et al.1 8 encapsulated CDs in the EuDPA-MOF $\mathrm{\langleDPA}=2,6$ -pyridinedicarboxylic acid) framework. The addition of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ resulted in the luminescence quenching of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ , while the fluorescence intensity of CDs remained unchanged. With the characteristic emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in EuDPA-MOF and the characteristic emission of CDs as probe and reference signal respectively, a ratiometric $(F_{425}/\bar{F}_{615})$ fluorescence sensor was constructed for the detection of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ . Wang et al.129 encapsulated CDs which codoped with nitrogen and cobalt in Eu-MOFs. For $\mathrm{CD}s@\mathrm{Eu-MOF}.$ , the blue luminescence of CDs at $467~\mathrm{\nm}$ was quenched with the increase of the chromium concentration of heavy metal cation pollutant, while the fluorescence intensity of $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ in Eu-MOFs remained unchanged at $616~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . A colorimetric fluorescence sensor $(F_{467}/F_{616})$ was constructed to detect $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ in water. The existence of MOF can not only stabilize CDs but also adsorb and accumulate $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ , which improves the sensitivity and selectivity of the probe. In addition, the detection of antibiotics and biomarkers using this ratio probe is also introduced. Fu et al.130 introduced ethylenediamine modified CDs into the luminescent Eu-MOF skeleton by a simple synthetic method (Figure 5F). The red luminescence intensity of the synthesized $\mathrm{CD}s@\mathrm{MOF}(\mathrm{Eu})$ increases with the increase of doxycycline concentration at $616~\mathrm{\nm}$ , while the blue luminescence intensity of CDs at $436~\mathrm{nm}$ is quenched (Figure 5G). The fluorescence change can be detected by the naked eye under a $365~\mathrm{nm}$ ultraviolet lamp (Figure 5H). Qin et al.131 doped carbon dots and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ into MOF material (MIL-53- COOH). With the addition of diaminotoluene (TDA), the luminous intensity of $I_{\mathrm{Eu}}=616~\mathrm{nm}$ for ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ /C-dots@MIL-53- COOH decreased obviously, while the luminous intensity of $I_{\mathrm{c-dots}}=440\ \mathrm{nm}$ changed little. A self-calibration dual-emission probe was constructed and used to detect early asthma marker TDA.
In addition to using the dual emission of lanthanide ions and carbon quantum dots to construct a ratiometric fluorescence sensor, Wu et al.132 used MOFs and carbon dots as luminescent guests and prepared a ratio fluorescent probe based on blue-emitting UiO-66- $(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}$ and red-emitting RBF-CQDs for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ detection (Figure 5I). Ma et al.133 encapsulated luminescent QDs and CDs in ZIF-8 structure with good fluorescence properties by the “one-pot method” and prepared a ratiometric probe for detecting $\mathrm{C\hat{u}}^{\hat{2}+}$ content in water.
Perovskite Quantum Dots. In recent years, perovskite quantum dots (PeQDs) have attracted much attention because of their excellent luminescent properties, such as high quantum yield, adjustable emission wavelength, narrow emission band, etc.134,135 However, they have poor chemical stability under environmental conditions and are sensitive to moisture, light, and temperature.136 Therefore, perovskite quantum dots are encapsulated in metal−organic framework materials to improve their stability, and some applications of fluorescence sensing are introduced.
For example, in the research of improving the stability of perovskite QDs, He et al.137 encapsulated high-quality $\mathbf{MAPbBr}_{3}$ perovskite quantum dots in metal−organic framework ZJU-28 $\mathrm{(ZJU-2\bar{8}~\ensuremath{~=~}~\left[I n_{3}(B T B)_{4}\right](M e_{2}N H_{2})_{3})}$ crystal, which can effectively reduce the quenching caused by aggregation and developed solid-state luminescence of nanomaterials. The resultant $\mathrm{ZJU-28\mathrm{\supsetMAPbBr}}_{3}$ has a high fluorescence lifetime, high quantum yield, and excellent photostability due to the protective effect of ZJU-28. Ren et al.138 encapsulated perovskite quantum dots $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}$ PeQDs 1 $\mathrm{{\widetilde{X}=C l},}$ Br, and I) with excellent optical properties into the pores of MOF-5. Because the size of perovskite quantum dots is larger than the pore size of MOF-5, the pore size of MOF-5 is expanded by using a supramolecular template, thus providing conditions for the encapsulation of PeQDs into MOF-5. The synthesized $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ has good stability and luminous performance.
Separately, in recent studies, it was found that some perovskite quantum dots encapsulated in MOF have a fluorescence response to temperature. Liu et al.139 encapsulated the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite quantum dot in the lanthanide metal−organic framework (Eu-BTC). The synthesized CsPbBr3@Eu-BTC has two photoluminescence properties of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ QDs and ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ . In the temperature range of 20−100 ${}^{\circ}{\bf C},$ the green emission intensity of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ QDs decreases with the increase of temperature, while the red emission of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ increases with the increase of temperature. A selfcalibration temperature sensor is realized. However, the energy transfer efficiency from ligand to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ in Eu-BTC is not high, which leads to low quantum yield and poor stability. Therefore, Ren et al.140 encapsulated the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ perovskite quantum dots into the blue-emitting ZJU-28 crystal which has a stable pore structure and strong fluorescence intensity (Figure 5J). Because the energy of ZJU-28 will transfer to $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ QDs and the ZJU-28 structure limits the nonradiative transition of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ QDs, the luminous quantum yield of perovskite quantum dots is greatly increased to $62\%$ . Under the excitation of ultraviolet light at $365~\mathrm{nm}$ , the synthesized $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}@\mathrm{ZJU}-28$ has two luminescence characteristics: blue emission of ZJU-28 at $442~\mathrm{nm}$ and green emission of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ QDs at $518~\mathrm{nm}$ . In the temperature range of $20{-}160\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , their fluorescence intensity has a good linear relationship with temperature. Therefore, they can be used to construct excellent temperature sensors. Zhang et al.141 encapsulated the luminescent $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbBr}_{3}$ perovskite QDs into MOF-5 crystal by a two-step method, thus improving the water stability and thermal stability of quantum dots. The synthesized $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}@\mathrm{MOF}\cdot5$ has stable photoluminescence in a wide $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ range. In the temperature range of 30−230 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the fluorescence intensity of the quantum dots can be quenched with the increase of temperature. In addition, metal ions ( $\mathrm{\mathop{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ) also cause a fluorescence response change in aqueous solution. ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ will react with surface ligands in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbBr}_{3}$ QDs to produce fluorescence quenching (Figure 5K), while $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ shows fluorescence sensitization (Figure 5L).
Luminescent Complex. Metal cation complexes such as iridium and ruthenium complexes have stable fluorescence in water and organic solutions. Recently, they are encapsulated in metal−organic framework materials as luminescent guests, which have certain applications in photocatalysis, white light emission, biomedicine, and fluorescence detection. Chen et al.142 encapsulated the red-emitting cationic complex Ru$\left(\log{9}\right)_{3}{^{2+}}$ on UiO-67 nanoparticles (Figure 6A) and synthesized a novel nano MOF light-emitting device for two-photon fluorescence imaging and photodynamic therapy. The quantum yield, two-photon luminescence intensity, luminescence life, and stability of the synthesized nanoparticles have been greatly improved due to the space limitation of MOF pores. The luminescence MOFs have good stability and dispersibility in aqueous solution and cell culture medium, providing a new way for clinical medicine in the future. Sun et al.143 encapsulated the iridium complex $\mathrm{[Ir(ppy)_{2}(b p y)]^{+}}$ with yellow fluorescence emission in the pores of MOF materials with blue luminescence emission. By adjusting the concentration of $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ , high-quality white light was observed (Figure 6B), and high quantum yield and optimized white light emission were effectively realized. Here, we mainly introduce the encapsulation of the luminescence complex into MOFs for fluorescence detection. For example, Yin et al.144 encapsulated the red-emitting cationic complex $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}{}^{2+}$ in the blue-emitting $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ hole by the “one-pot method”. The blue fluorescence intensity increases with the increase of water content in organic solution, while the red fluorescence emission intensity is relatively stable (Figure 6C). $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ is encapsulated stably in MOF pores, which makes the sensor have the advantages of high sensitivity, low detection limit, wide detection range, fast response, strong stability, and reusability. Zhu et al.145 encapsulated the cationic complex $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ in the framework of UiO-66 coated with manganese dioxide nanosheets $\left(\mathrm{MnO}_{2}\mathrm{NSs}\right)$ . The existence of $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ NSs inhibited the release of $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)_{3}}}^{2+},$ , thus quenching its fluorescence. $\mathbf{MnO}_{2}$ will be oxidized to $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ when GSH is added, so that the fluorescence intensity of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\ensuremath{\mathrm{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ -UiO-66 will be restored (Figure 6D). In addition, UiO-66 can enhance the fluorescence of $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ . The complex $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{NS}@\$ $\mathrm{{Ru}}(\mathrm{{bpy}})_{3}^{2+}$ -UiO-66 can accurately detect glutathione in cancer cells with a detection limit of $0.28~\mu\mathrm{M}$ . Zhao et al.146 encapsulated green luminescence $\big[\mathrm{Ir}\big(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{-PPY-F}_{2}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{bpy}\big)\big]^{+}$ and red luminescence $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ in blue light-emitting anionic MOF material $\left(\mathrm{ME}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\left[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{L})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\right]$ ·4DMA. The existence of volatile organic solution will change the energy transfer from MOF to two cationic complexes, thus leading to the color of the complex changing from blue to white. In the detection of nitro aromatic hydrocarbon (NAC) vapor, the color of the complex is obviously darkened. A novel multidimensional ratio luminescence probe was developed by tuning red, green, and blue luminescence. The luminescent probe has the advantages of high sensitivity, high selectivity, and low cost to realize fluorescence detection in volatile organic solution and nitroaromatic hydrocarbon vapor.
The advantages and disadvantages of the above-mentioned four luminescent guests for the construction of luminescent MOFs were compared below. Lanthanide metal ions are attractive for luminescent guests owing to their large Stokes shifts, narrow emission peak, and long luminescence lifetimes. Due to the fact that the 4f−4f electronic transition of the lanthanide metal ion is transition forbidden, the molar absorptivity of the lanthanide metal ions is relatively low, so the lanthanide metal ions cannot be efficiently excited by the excitation light and generate higher external quantum efficiency, and can only depend on the sensitization of the organic ligands to have relatively strong fluorescence emission. In addition, the lanthanides currently used to construct luminescent MOFs are of limited variety and the lanthanides have limited reserves on earth, thus hindering the wide application of lanthanide MOFs. Fluorescent dyes, as cheap and easily available luminescent guests, have attracted much attention because of their high photoluminescence quantum yield, stable optical properties, and wide emission wavelength range. However, the dyes are easy to aggregate and greatly weaken their luminous intensity; moreover, the fluorescent intensity of organic dyes may be affected by $\mathsf{p H}$ and environmental conditions. Introducing fluorescent dyes into the pores of MOFs can protect fluorescent dyes and avoid fluorescence aggregation quenching. Its poor photobleaching cannot be ignored. Compared with fluorescent dyes, quantum dots have received considerable attention due to their photobleaching stability, elevated photostability, and wide excitation and narrow emission of their fluorescence spectrum. However, in order to improve the stability and luminous quantum yield of QDs encapsulated in MOFs, it is necessary to modify QDs sometimes, because modification with a capping agent can prevent aggregation of quantum dots, thus increasing the complexity of operation. Furthermore, the size of quantum dots should be controlled to be smaller than the pore size of MOFs. If it is larger than the pore size, then the pore size of MOFs should be expanded to encapsulation of QDs into MOFs. Most inorganic semiconductive QDs contain toxic elements such as cadmium and lead; it may be toxic to organisms when they are used for biological detection. Luminescent complexes such as iridium and ruthenium are well-known for their high photoluminescence efficiency, relatively short lifetimes, and emissive light variety. Importantly, the size of these complexes is small and they can be well encapsulated in the pore size of MOFs, thus effectively reducing the quenching phenomenon caused by aggregation. However, there are also a few luminescent complexes currently used to construct luminescent MOFs because of a lack of variety.
## CONCLUSION AND OUTLOOK
This paper introduces the encapsulation of several kinds of typical luminous guests including lanthanide ions, dyes, quantum dots, and luminescent complexes into the metal organic skeleton to construct LMOFs which have a single- or double-launch luminescent center for fluorescence sensing (Table 1). These sensors have been successfully applied to the chemistry, biology, and environment of species sensing, including cationic, anionic, gas molecules, explosives, temperature, humidity, $\mathrm{\pH},$ and biomarkers.
However, although the constructed LMOF materials have made great progress in fluorescence detection, some challenges still remain to be solved. For example, in terms of synthesis, the luminescent property of LMOFs mainly depends on the framework structure of the MOF material and the synergy between photonic units. The main limitation of the framework structure is that the pore size, structure, metal node, and organic ligand properties of MOFs must be matched with the size, surface charge, and intrinsic properties of the fluorophore. Synergy refers to the relationship between the synergistic effects of different photonic units such as metal nodes, organic connectors, and guest molecules. Therefore, we need to deeply study the relationship between the structure and properties, preparation strategy, and the host−guest interaction and synergistic effect of LMOFs. The combination of experimental research and theoretical calculation will further promote the development of LMOFs. In terms of detection, most detection processes are based on the “turn-off” process, while the “turnon”-based LMOF material can improve selectivity and sensitivity.147,148 At the same time, most of the established LMOF sensors rely on the variation of the intensity of the emission signal from a single-emission center, which may cause an erroneous response due to the variation of the external conditions. The ratio-type fluorescent probe and selfcalibration method can eliminate environmental interference and improve the detection accuracy. In addition, the development of a multidimensional sensing strategy utilizing intensity, lifetime, wavelength, and polarization may be the future development direction for improving the sensing accuracy. In terms of application, most analytes are detected in aqueous solutions, especially in biological samples, which requires a high water stability of the detection materials. Therefore, the solubility and stability of the LMOF sensor need to be improved. For in vivo sensing, biocompatibility needs to be considered, while the toxicity study on the LMOF sensor is still rare. Therefore, nontoxic photon units can be more selected to construct the LMOF materials.13 In addition, the versatility of the LMOF composite needs to be further explored for its optical imaging, diagnostic, and drug release properties in biotherapy. We believe that, with continuous research and improvement, the bright future of LMOFs in the field of fluorescence detection can be expected.
