## VOCABULARY
Fluorescent probe, a fluorophore (fluorescent dye) that can respond to specific stimuli (such as temperature, $\mathrm{\tt{pH}},$ , specific substances or ions, etc.) or locate specific areas of biological specimens; radiation energy, the energy of thermal radiation or various electromagnetic waves (usually refers to the visible light and infrared, ultraviolet energy); Stokes shift, the fluorescence spectrum moves to the long wavelength direction compared with the absorption spectrum; photoluminescence, the phenomenon that molecules or materials could emit specific light upon absorption of incoming light (generally including fluorescence and phosphoresce); synergy, the addition or blending of two or more components together, so that the generated effect is greater than the sum of the effects of each component when used alone; signal-to-noise ratio, the ratio of the fluctuation of the analyzed signal to that of the blank signal (noise)
